        <h1>Mars Curiosity Rover: Exploring the Red Planet</h1>
        
        <h2>Launch and Mission Timeline</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Launch Date:</strong> November 26, 2011</li>
            <li><strong>Landing on Mars:</strong> August 6, 2012</li>
            <li><strong>First Image Transmission:</strong> August 6, 2012</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            After a 354-million-mile journey, Curiosity landed in <strong>Gale Crater</strong> using NASA's innovative Sky Crane system. 
            Just minutes after landing, it sent back its first images of the Martian surface.
        </p>
        
        <h2>Capturing the Martian Surface</h2>
        <p>
            Curiosity is equipped with advanced scientific instruments, including the <strong>Mast Camera (MastCam)</strong>, 
            <strong>Mars Hand Lens Imager (MAHLI)</strong>, and <strong>Navigation Cameras (Navcams)</strong>. These cameras capture high-resolution images of Mars' terrain, 
            providing insights into the planet's geology and climate history.
        </p>
        
        <h2>Notable Discoveries</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Evidence of ancient <strong>riverbeds and lake deposits</strong></li>
            <li>Detection of <strong>organic molecules</strong> and key chemical elements</li>
            <li>Observations of <strong>seasonal methane variations</strong> in the atmosphere</li>
        </ul>
        
        <h2>Ongoing Mission</h2>
        <p>
            Originally planned for a <strong>two-year mission</strong>, Curiosity has exceeded expectations and continues operating over a decade later.
            It keeps sending back breathtaking images and valuable data, helping scientists prepare for future human exploration of Mars.
        </p>
        <p>
            As of today, Curiosity remains one of NASA's most successful and enduring missions on Mars.
        </p>
